WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Luminosity is the energy a star emits each second.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.400
Apparent brightness is the energy arriving each second per

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.220
square meter. Watch the same outgoing light spread across

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.000
larger spheres. Double the distance while keeping luminosity fixed.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.360
The sphere has four times the area, so a

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.540
detector receives one quarter as much light. Triple the

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.520
distance gives one ninth. Equal apparent brightness does not

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.460
mean equal luminosity. A star twice as far away

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.360
needs four times the luminosity to look equally bright.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.488
These comparisons assume no intervening extinction.
